Square $y=\sin\theta\cos\theta$:
$$\begin{align*}
y^{2} &= \sin^{2}\theta\cos^{2}\theta \\
9y^{2} &= 9(1-\cos^{2}\theta)\cos^{2}\theta \\
&= 3(3\cos^{2}\theta) - (3\cos^{2}\theta)^{2}
\end{align*}$$
Substitute $x=3\cos^{2}\theta$:
$$\begin{align*}
9y^{2} &= 3x - x^{2} \\
y &= \pm\frac{\sqrt{3x - x^{2}}}{3}
\end{align*}$$
Since $\theta\in\left[0,\frac{\pi}{2}\right]$, both $\sin\theta\ge 0$ and $\cos\theta\ge 0$, so $y\ge 0$. Take the positive root:
$$y=\frac{\sqrt{3x-x^{2}}}{3}$$
